ce Your Hormones*</h5></span></div><div data-rid="36748fa0-b766-4fb4-9dc5-d4d82f47fc4e" class="r-f3cm9e"><div data-rid="e38181dc-7747-46d1-9b41-dab923214f2b" class="r-a7z9ov" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="967e19e2-d522-44fb-a81a-3046fc835956" aria-controls="replo-:R1vdolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1uwffqa"><div data-rid="a27bc412-717f-4b6d-94c2-380561691861" class="r-18ly6fn alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>What happens?</p></span></div><div data-rid="21d2a0a9-dc5b-4c6f-8b0b-efec246fd269" class="r-13ca1t"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1oidivt" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1vdolr2:"><div data-rid="c05411e8-79a8-492e-a2fc-95c8542985a4" class="r-12ttty7"><div data-rid="732eb859-f60a-41d0-b861-bce8cacd47c4" class="r-kmrwsr alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Your hormones naturally rise and fall each month. But when oestrogen rises too high (often under everyday stress, hectic schedules, bad sleep, or poor diet), it triggers an overproduction of prostaglandins — the main driver behind deeper cramps and inflammation.<br><br>The result? With this natural balance lost, you´re left facing heightened cramping, and fewer defenses against pain flares. f you don’t have a condition like PCOS or endometriosis, studies indicate that the difference between someone with debilitating cramps and someone who barely notices theirs comes down to prostaglandin levels.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="a57d32b8-e1ba-4d16-a872-2c6fdb64893d" class="r-1c8ai1h" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="3d0c0bca-ad6c-4d9f-a5f6-8f712c07279e" aria-controls="replo-:R2vdolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1srxp3i"><div data-rid="c4665bd6-ff91-403c-975d-a876fa9e0d7b" class="r-1m30d20 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>How monthlies helps</p></span></div><div data-rid="d695b9e7-f63e-48d4-9c4b-8fbc9d8f500b" class="r-19qaig6"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1jqbiwg" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R2vdolr2:"><div data-rid="0409dabe-5a0b-4ec4-865a-8cc6ffc917f9" class="r-12f7ql9"><div data-rid="99cdcbbc-f985-4e4e-9363-029f60a758a9" class="r-wepymr alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Formulated with scientifically backed ingredients like <strong>Lemon Balm, Broccoli Seed Extract, B-Complex, Myo-Inositol, Choline, and Flaxseed</strong>, monthlies supports hormonal balance at its core—optimising oestrogen metabolism, promoting healthy progesterone, and supporting stress management.*<br><br>As your hormonal pathways are optimised, you’re less prone to overactive cramping and inflammation from the start, <strong>setting the stage for cramps you barely even notice.*</strong></p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="d282f6ef-e898-4fa9-9b17-f91053e7450b" class="r-mfw1mu" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="ede8541e-23a6-44ec-9d63-acf63c064d54" aria-controls="replo-:R3vdolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-x8rn97"><div data-rid="ee29f1eb-fec8-4560-81a5-76367bfc115d" class="r-sznd7i alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Clinical study highlight</p></span></div><div data-rid="183ed04c-3de3-4d7f-8cd8-f1952f20384c" class="r-co2tks"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1xkx84k" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R3vdolr2:"><div data-rid="eae2db40-a893-456d-88d7-1281057cdaf1" class="r-1bc2c7p"><div data-rid="3aaba5ff-3ab7-4845-9aa6-63b702527765" class="r-1un9fng alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Lemon Balm (found in monthlies): A double-blind randomized, placebo-controlled trial was performed on 100 high school girls and found that after 3 months, Lemon balm decreased PMS intensity scores by 67.3%. In another double-blinded placebo-controlled trial, 87% of participants in the treated group reported improved sleep quality, compared to 30% in the placebo group.*</p></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-state="inactive" data-orientation="vertical" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="radix-:Rolr2:-trigger-8a6e8634-2204-481a-825d-38a7f86ad1691" id="radix-:Rolr2:-content-8a6e8634-2204-481a-825d-38a7f86ad1691" tabindex="0" style="display:none"><div data-rid="fc1d07a2-7f1e-47d7-b034-fd03b9ed6d05" class="r-59mlhw"><div data-rid="e25ba560-4bdb-4cd8-97da-2528e608d763" class="r-135iioh"><div data-rid="fdbb172a-7327-41da-b649-4a0608063cc0" class="r-14ht610"><div data-rid="bbe1bca1-7a50-4786-909f-eb3ac22df275" class="r-11ttoxx"><picture data-rid="4c4318bf-d8e0-4c91-b09b-e41b3ed0e2fb" class="r-yfkf6c"><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0708/7462/8371/files/v3_1_a36178d0-95b9-4783-ac76-8038a7e2e39f.webp?v=1739282201&amp;width=820" media="(max-width: 640px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0615/3951/6642/files/Frame_9914145.webp?v=1720629119&amp;width=1024" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"/><source srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0615/3951/6642/files/Frame_9914145.webp?v=1720629119&amp;width=1800" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"/><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0615/3951/6642/files/Frame_9914145.webp?v=1720629119" alt="Supplement bottle in a garden, highlighting ginger, turmeric, and aniseed extracts." class="r-1lx6z6" loading="lazy"/></picture><div data-rid="779fad28-306f-4823-ad4e-ccb56c11c426" class="r-37pa6l alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h5>Tame Prostaglandins*</h5></span></div><div data-rid="ac06324a-275e-45b7-9609-c6a8abbbf123" class="r-uqy31z"><div data-rid="04deac2d-c7fc-4de2-8298-681ce786de62" class="r-1jt2tdx" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="a7a9d03e-d814-41c2-a025-fea9251c3f5d" aria-controls="replo-:R1vlolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1dst5ru"><div data-rid="1ea78213-d483-401e-b49a-24ca37b807a1" class="r-126xk41 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>What happens?</p></span></div><div data-rid="c80633d8-c591-40a1-b4ee-8ab49dd47949" class="r-bksrx1"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-14idlnk" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1vlolr2:"><div data-rid="7bf449c4-25a1-487a-95b4-0bb7f1a823c1" class="r-hidntt"><div data-rid="036be8aa-ecb1-42cf-a9c6-62d0a3ddde7a" class="r-1lperyl alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Prostaglandins are essential and help your uterus shed its lining. However, when they surge — due to an an overactive COX-2 enzyme — they directly intensify cramps, sharpen pain signals, and make them more frequent.</p><p><br></p><p>Painkillers work by blocking prostaglandins after they’ve spiked, often hitting COX-1 (needed for stomach protection) along with COX-2. This can lead to side effects like indigestion or ulcers —while leaving hormonal and nutrient imbalances unresolved, so the cycle of pain keeps repeating.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="fca5b837-1644-46ee-a7a5-905985b85c55" class="r-t898ov" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="6e588bb5-b218-4179-8711-27e555f7a4d2" aria-controls="replo-:R2vlolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-uxqtf4"><div data-rid="88c0e552-8149-424f-ba54-e4975a4d8bb2" class="r-qxdytf alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>How monthlies helps</p></span></div><div data-rid="7591ecfd-fbd8-4e51-9ccc-b4e9adc17091" class="r-ruxl6j"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-15ry1zc" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R2vlolr2:"><div data-rid="44332686-85c4-428a-86eb-73dafb509b2f" class="r-175xgqk"><div data-rid="59739fcd-2c5b-4c61-9649-74e2f91d631a" class="r-13rizw0 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Formulated with <strong>potent anti-inflammatory</strong> support from Ginger Root Extract, Turmeric, and Aniseed, monthlies helps combat inflammation— with Ginger and Turmeric <strong>selectively favoring COX-2 inhibition to safeguard your stomach lining.*</strong><br>         <br>And because monthlies is designed for daily use — unlike painkillers — it works to keep COX-2 in check all month long, <strong>helping manage discomfort during your period while also addressing pain before it even begins.*<br>         </strong><br>By calming pain amplifiers at their source, monthlies supports less intense contractions, lower inflammation, and helps ease the nerve sensitivity that magnifies cramps — <strong>without the side effects you can get from painkillers.*</strong></p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="11dfafd9-624c-4d0d-865c-1e63a3729daa" class="r-1wu0lrh" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="0464cc5f-20b4-4fa6-9c44-7ee3f0df5b56" aria-controls="replo-:R3vlolr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1c86geo"><div data-rid="9c7f7ba0-80d6-469c-84b1-e863a365a758" class="r-13g7xk6 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Clinical study highlight</p></span></div><div data-rid="df5cde16-27b1-47af-8d93-03f00ee494e8" class="r-8ntab6"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="presentation" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-parchy" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M1.646 4.646a.5.5 0 0 1 .708 0L8 10.293l5.646-5.647a.5.5 0 0 1 .708.708l-6 6a.5.5 0 0 1-.708 0l-6-6a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.708"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R3vlolr2:"><div data-rid="735a27ee-8e2b-4304-9260-5749f4b06b17" class="r-tc2jlz"><div data-rid="8226fdbd-80a1-48ed-9696-8d682edbf807" class="r-1gpml89 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Ginger (found in Monthlies): A systematic review of 11 studies found there was no significant difference between ginger and NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) in decreasing pain intensity during menstruation. Additionally, studies showed that ginger reduced menstrual bleeding volume to the same degree as Mefenamic acid, a common pain medication. This suggests ginger offers comparable relief to pharmaceutical options without their side effects.*</p></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-state="inactive" data-orientation="vertical" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="radix-:Rolr2:-trigger-5c7d1767-2810-48ae-aaf9-e61a1f0d08c12" id="radix-:Rolr2:-content-5c7d1767-2810-48ae-aaf9-e61a1f0d08c12" tabindex="0" style="display:none"><div data-rid="3c33fbe2-73d3-4f47-9d57-bd2f245b080c" class="r-1oa